---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const testCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · State H₀ and H₁', note: 'H₀ (null): nothing is going on — the drug does nothing, the coin is fair. H₁: something is. You can only ever reject H₀, never "prove" H₁.' },
    { math: '2 · Choose α (usually 0.05)', note: 'The false-positive rate you tolerate: 5% chance of crying wolf when nothing is real. This is a design decision, not a discovery.' },
    { math: '3 · Compute the p-value', note: 'The probability of seeing data this extreme IF H₀ were true. It is NOT the probability that H₀ is true — the single most common misreading in science.' },
    { math: '4 · Reject or don\'t — then think', note: 'p < α: reject H₀, report effect size and interval. p > α: insufficient evidence — not "proof of no effect". Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence.' },
  ],
};

const testQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    {
      q: 'A study reports p = 0.03 for a new teaching method. The correct reading is:',
      choices: [
        'There is a 3% chance the method does nothing',
        'If the method truly did nothing, data at least this favourable would appear about 3% of the time — surprising enough under the null to reject it at the 5% level',
        'The method is 97% likely to work',
        'The study is 3% likely to be fraudulent',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'The p-value is computed assuming the null is true; it cannot then be the probability that the null is true. It measures the surprise of the data under "nothing is going on", not the probability of "nothing is going on" under the data — the direction of the conditional is the whole content of the method.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Two events are mutually exclusive versus independent. Which statement is true?',
      choices: [
        'They mean the same thing',
        'Mutually exclusive means they cannot both happen (P(A and B) = 0); independent means one gives no information about the other (P(A and B) = P(A)×P(B)) — and two events with positive probability cannot be both, since if A happens, a mutually exclusive B cannot',
        'Independent events cannot happen together',
        'Mutually exclusive events have probability 1/2 each',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'This is the classic exam trap. "Rain and snow today" are neither exclusive nor independent; "rolling a six" and "rolling a four" are mutually exclusive but enormously dependent (one forbids the other); "two different people are left-handed" are independent. Exclusive is about the outcome list; independence is about information.',
    },
    {
      q: 'A test screens for a disease affecting 1 in 100, detecting it 99% of the time, with a 5% false-positive rate. You test positive. Your chance of having the disease is roughly:',
      choices: [
        '99%',
        'About 1 in 6 (16–17%) — the rare-disease base rate dominates: among 10,000 people, ~99 true positives share the result with ~495 false positives (tree diagram arithmetic)',
        '50%',
        '5%',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'The tree diagram settles it: of 10,000, about 100 are diseased, ~99 detected; the 9,900 healthy contribute ~495 false positives. P(disease | positive) = 99 ÷ (99 + 495) ≈ 0.167. Conditional probability runs from evidence back to cause — and ignoring the base rate is the most consequential error in applied probability.',
    },
    {
      q: 'A well-powered study finds p = 0.31 for a small effect. The correct conclusion is:',
      choices: [
        'The effect does not exist',
        'Insufficient evidence at the chosen level — not proof of nothing: report the confidence interval on the effect size ("between −0.4 and +1.2 points"), which shows what the study can and cannot rule out',
        'The p-value was computed wrong',
        'The study should be repeated until p drops below 0.05',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence — but the interval is what makes the statement quantitative: if the 95% CI spans zero yet barely excludes the effect that matters practically, more data is needed; if it excludes everything useful, the question is genuinely closed. Repeating until significance is p-hacking, not evidence.',
    },
  ],
};

const htComplEst = {
  "prompt": "A fair die, rolled three times. What is the chance of at least one six, in percent?",
  "unit": "%",
  "answer": 42,
  "factor": 1.2,
  "why": "1 − (5/6)³ ≈ 0.42 — the complement rule turns 'at least one' into a single clean path instead of a pile of cases.",
  "context": "Complements always sum to 1: P(A′) = 1 − P(A)."
};

const htHypSort = {
  "prompt": "Which claims are defaults to be overturned, and which are what a result would establish?",
  "buckets": [
    "a job for H₀ — the 'nothing' default",
    "a job for H₁ — what a result would establish"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "the coin is fair",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "the drug matches the placebo",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "the new timetable makes no difference",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "the coin is biased",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "the drug works",
      "bucket": 1
    }
  ],
  "why": "'Nothing is going on' is usually precise and computable, while 'something is going on' is vague — surprise is computed against the precise claim, and the null is never proved, only survived."
};

const htCoinSpot = {
  "prompt": "One line of this worked test is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "H₀: the coin is fair (p = 0.5); H₁: the coin is biased (p ≠ 0.5 — two-sided, since suspicion was flagged before knowing the direction).",
    "α = 0.05 — the false-alarm rate tolerated, chosen before the flips.",
    "P(exactly 9 heads in 10 fair flips) = C(10,9) × 0.5¹⁰ = 10/1024; P(10 heads) = 1/1024.",
    "The p-value = (10 + 1)/1024 ≈ 0.0107 — one direction only.",
    "Verdict: 0.0215 < 0.05, so reject H₀ and report the effect honestly."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "The suspicion was two-sided, so the p-value is 2 × (10 + 1)/1024 = 22/1024 ≈ 0.0215 — both directions counted, which is exactly what line 5 compares against α.",
  "why": "The test never computes the probability that the coin is unfair — that would need a prior. It quantifies how embarrassing the data is for fairness."
};

const pvalMatch = {
  "prompt": "Match each misreading to the honest reply:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "“p is the probability H₀ is true”",
      "right": "No — it is computed assuming H₀; P(evidence | cause) and P(cause | evidence) came out 99% versus 16.7% on the disease tree"
    },
    {
      "left": "“p = 0.03 means 97% chance of replication”",
      "right": "No — replication depends on the true effect size and the repeat study's power"
    },
    {
      "left": "“p > 0.05 proves no effect”",
      "right": "No — the data did not clear the bar; the study may simply have been too small"
    },
    {
      "left": "“p = 0.049 is real, p = 0.051 is not”",
      "right": "No — the gap is noise-level trivial; α is a convention and p is a continuous measure of surprise"
    }
  ],
  "why": "The ASA's 2016 statement catalogued exactly these errors: a p-value indicates how incompatible the data are with a specified model — it measures nothing else."
};

const htDiscEst = {
  "prompt": "Testing 1,000 drug candidates of which 50 truly work, at α = 0.05 with 80% power: roughly how many false 'discoveries' do you expect?",
  "unit": "discoveries",
  "answer": 48,
  "factor": 1.3,
  "why": "950 true nulls × 0.05 ≈ 48 false discoveries beside 50 × 0.8 = 40 true ones — over half the significant results are wrong, purely from base rates.",
  "context": "This is why multiple-comparisons discipline, Bonferroni's α/n, pre-registration and replication exist."
};

const htSigJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "'Not significant' proves the effect does not exist.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "A large, careful trial can return p = 0.2 for a small-but-real effect — the study may simply lack power."
    },
    {
      "s": "The confidence interval on the effect shows precisely what the study could and could not rule out.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "'Somewhere between −0.1 and +0.9 points' converts the verdict into a quantitative statement."
    },
    {
      "s": "Absence of evidence is evidence of absence.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "The p-value only says the data did not clear the bar — the interval is what says what was actually excluded."
    }
  ]
};

const htEffCompare = {
  "prompt": "Both p-values cleared 0.05 — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "significant but clinically trivial",
    "text": "a drug that lowers blood pressure by 0.1 mmHg — p = 0.01, n = 50,000 — is a 'so what?' once the effect size is in units that matter"
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "significant and huge",
    "text": "pair every p with an effect size and a confidence interval — the previous lesson's error bar on it — and the two stories separate"
  },
  "why": "A lone p-value under-reports its own story: one number about one comparison under one model."
};

const htAlphaBranch = {
  "root": "α is a priced decision, not a statistical constant. Walk the desk.",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "Screening a blood donation for a dangerous virus. What bar do you set?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "α very low, plus confirmatory testing",
          "next": "screen",
          "feedback": "A false negative contaminates the supply — the costs are asymmetric and catastrophic in one direction."
        },
        {
          "label": "0.05 — the universal convention",
          "next": "trap",
          "feedback": "The convention is not a constant here; the price of each error decides."
        }
      ]
    },
    "screen": {
      "text": "Next file: an A/B test of a button colour. What bar?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "0.05 is generous — speed wins",
          "next": "ab",
          "feedback": "Both errors cost pennies; waiting costs more than either error does."
        },
        {
          "label": "α = 0.001 — buttons are safety-critical",
          "next": "trap",
          "feedback": "Overkill — the economics do not justify the width."
        }
      ]
    },
    "ab": {
      "text": "Filed. And a comet-sampling spacecraft gets evidence standards so extreme that a false positive can never waste a billion-euro mission."
    },
    "trap": {
      "text": "Mispriced — reporting a single p-value without saying what each error would have cost reports the arithmetic while hiding the economics."
    }
  },
  "why": "The significance level belongs to the decision, not the mathematics — blood, buttons and spacecraft set it differently."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/intermediate/hypothesis-testing">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson is where the statistics track stops describing and starts deciding.
    <strong>Describing Data</strong> handed you centres and spreads; <strong>Probability
    Foundations</strong> made randomness computable — and this page leans on it hard,
    because the whole test procedure is written in probability's grammar: notation, the
    addition rule, independence, conditional probability, tree diagrams. <strong>Confidence
    Intervals</strong> supplied the error bar this page keeps citing; here the same
    mathematics asks a sharper question — is the effect real at all?
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>run the probability grammar: complements, with and without replacement, the addition rule, independence and conditional probability — including the classic disease-test tree;</li>
    <li>set up a test honestly: H₀, H₁ and a significance level fixed before the data is seen;</li>
    <li>work a small two-sided test by hand, from binomial probabilities to a reject-or-not verdict;</li>
    <li>state what a p-value is, and defuse the misreadings that corrupted a century of published science;</li>
    <li>name the two error types, explain their trade-off, and price a significance level to the decision it serves.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Hypothesis testing is statistics's courtroom. The defendant is the <strong>null hypothesis
    (H₀)</strong> — "nothing interesting is happening" — and data either provides evidence beyond
    reasonable doubt (reject) or fails to (stay with H₀). The <strong>p-value</strong> is the
    strength of surprise under H₀: how unlikely is this data if nothing were going on?
    The whole procedure rests on the probability toolkit — notation, the addition rule,
    independence, conditional probability, tree diagrams — so this page builds that
    foundation first, then assembles the test on top of it: the four steps, a worked
    test from raw data, the two errors named Type I and II, and the misreadings of the
    p-value that corrupted a century of published science. By the end you should be
    able to run a small test by hand and, more importantly, read one critically.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four steps — and the two misreadings that corrupt published science" widget="equationStep" config={testCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the panel walks the four steps in order, and the last
    one is the step beginners skip — "reject or don't, then think". Steps 1 and 2 are
    declarations made before any data exists; step 3 computes a conditional probability
    under H₀; step 4 turns the comparison into a verdict plus an honest report. Notice too
    what the panel never says: nowhere does it offer the probability that H₀ is true —
    that misreading is this page's main target.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the courtroom is staged — null, alternative, significance
    level, verdict — and the procedure rests entirely on probability's grammar. Next:
    that grammar itself, from complements to the tree diagram that diagnoses a positive
    test result.
  </p>

  <h2>Probability foundations: the grammar tests are written in</h2>
  <p>
    Probability notation compresses the reasoning: <strong>P(A)</strong> is the probability
    of event A; <strong>P(A′)</strong> (read "A-prime") is the probability of A
    <em>not</em> happening, and complements always sum to 1 — P(A′) = 1 − P(A),
    which is why "at least one…" questions are easiest backwards (P(at least one
    six in three rolls) = 1 − (5/6)³ ≈ 0.42, rather than enumerating cases).
    <strong>With and without replacement</strong> change the denominators: drawing
    two aces from a deck <em>with</em> replacement gives (4/52) × (4/52) = 1/169;
    <em>without</em> replacement the second draw inherits the first's depletion:
    (4/52) × (3/51) = 1/221. <strong>The addition rule</strong> handles "A or B":
    P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B), where the subtraction removes the
    double-counted overlap. When A and B cannot both happen — <strong>mutually
    exclusive</strong> — the overlap is empty and the rule collapses to plain
    addition. <strong>Independence</strong> is a different idea entirely: B is
    independent of A when learning A tells you nothing about B —
    P(B | A) = P(B) — which makes the multiplication rule
    P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B) legal. Mutually exclusive events with positive
    probability are therefore never independent (knowing one happened
    <em>forbids</em> the other — maximum information). Finally,
    <strong>conditional probability</strong>, P(A | B) — the probability of A given
    B — is the engine of every real inference, and the <strong>tree diagram</strong>
    is its machine: branch by the sequence of events, multiply along
    branches (chains of "and"), and add across the branches that match the
    condition. <strong>The classic worked tree:</strong> a disease affects 1 in 100
    people; a test detects it 99% of the time and false-positives 5% of the
    time. Out of 10,000 people: about 100 are diseased, contributing 99
    true positives; the 9,900 healthy contribute 9,900 × 0.05 = 495 false
    positives. So P(disease | positive) = 99 ÷ (99 + 495) ≈ <strong>0.167</strong> —
    a positive result on a "99% accurate" test leaves you with about a
    one-in-six chance. That reversal (from P(positive | disease) to
    P(disease | positive)) is the base-rate lesson, and hypothesis testing
    is its disciplined cousin.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="call the complement before enumerating" widget="estimate" config={htComplEst} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> conditional probability — evidence running backwards from
    observation to cause — is the engine underneath every test, and the disease-tree shows
    how badly untrained intuition handles it (a "99% accurate" test leaving a one-in-six
    chance). Next: the courtroom apparatus built on top of that grammar.
  </p>

  <h2>The courtroom: null and alternative</h2>
  <p>
    A hypothesis test asks: is this data surprising enough to overturn the
    default? The default — the <strong>null hypothesis H₀</strong> — is always the
    "nothing" claim: the coin is fair, the drug matches the placebo, the new
    timetable makes no difference. The <strong>alternative H₁</strong> is what a
    result would establish: the coin is biased, the drug works. The
    asymmetry is deliberate and mirrors the law: the null gets the benefit
    of the doubt, and evidence must be <em>strong enough to reject it</em> —
    you can never "accept" or prove the null, only fail to reject it.
    (The philosophical reason: "nothing is going on" is usually a precise,
    computable claim, while "something is going on" is vague — statistics
    computes surprise against the precise claim.) Choosing H₀ and H₁ is
    the step where honesty lives: they must be fixed from the question,
    before the data is seen — because the method's error guarantees
    (next section) only hold for tests declared in advance.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="file each claim with its hypothesis" widget="sort-buckets" config={htHypSort} />

  <h2>The four steps, with a worked test</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>A worked example.</strong> A friend claims a coin is fair. You flip it
    10 times and see 9 heads. Suspicious? <strong>Step 1:</strong> H₀: the coin is
    fair (p = 0.5); H₁: the coin is biased (p ≠ 0.5 — two-sided, since we
    flagged suspicion before knowing the direction). <strong>Step 2:</strong> choose
    the significance level α = 0.05 — the false-alarm rate we will tolerate.
    <strong>Step 3:</strong> compute the p-value — the probability, <em>if the coin were
    fair</em>, of data at least this extreme in either direction. By the
    binomial distribution: P(exactly 9 heads) = C(10,9) × 0.5¹⁰ = 10/1024,
    P(10 heads) = 1/1024, and symmetrically 9-or-10 tails; the two-sided
    p-value = 2 × (10 + 1)/1024 = 22/1024 ≈ <strong>0.0215</strong>.
    <strong>Step 4:</strong> compare: 0.0215 is under 0.05, so reject H₀ — the data
    would occur under a fair coin only about 2% of the time, below our
    false-alarm budget; the friend's coin earns serious suspicion. Notice
    what the test did and did not say: it did not compute the probability
    the coin is unfair (that would need a prior — the Bayesian lesson's
    territory); it quantified how embarrassing the data is for fairness.
    And notice the sample-size discipline hiding in the arithmetic: with
    only 10 flips, even 9 heads sits near the boundary — the same p-value
    logic with 100 flips and 90 heads would be utterly conclusive
    (p ≈ 10⁻¹⁸), because more data makes the null's predictions sharper.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="one line of the coin test is wrong — click it" widget="spot-error" config={htCoinSpot} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a test is four disciplined steps — declare the hypotheses,
    price the false alarm, compute the surprise, decide and report. The step everyone
    misreads is the third, so it earns its own section.
  </p>

  <h2>The p-value: what it is and, more importantly, is not</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>p-value</strong> is P(data at least this extreme | H₀ true) — the
    probability of the <em>data</em> given the <em>null</em>. The two great
    misreadings both flip that conditional or inflate it:
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="the four misreadings — pair each with its defusal" widget="match" config={pvalMatch} />
  <ul>
    <li><strong>"p is the probability H₀ is true."</strong> No — it is computed <em>assuming</em> H₀. The disease-test tree shows how different P(evidence | cause) and P(cause | evidence) can be (99% versus 16.7%).</li>
    <li><strong>"p = 0.03 means 97% chance of replication" (or of being right).</strong> No — replication depends on the true effect size and the power of the repeat study.</li>
    <li><strong>"p &gt; 0.05 proves no effect."</strong> No — it means the data did not clear the evidence bar; the study may simply have been too small.</li>
    <li><strong>"p = 0.049 is real, p = 0.051 is not."</strong> No — the difference between them is noise-level trivial; α is a convention, and the p-value is a continuous measure of surprise.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The American Statistical Association's 2016 statement — a rare
    institutional intervention — catalogued exactly these errors and
    reaffirmed the honest summary: a p-value can indicate how incompatible
    the data are with a specified model; it measures nothing else.
  </p>

  <h2>The two errors, named</h2>
  <p>
    Two ways to be wrong, and the procedure names them. <strong>Type I error</strong>:
    rejecting a true null — convicting the innocent, announcing a drug
    works when it does not. Its long-run rate is set by α: at α = 0.05,
    one test in twenty of <em>genuine nothing</em> cries wolf.
    <strong>Type II error</strong>: failing to reject a false null — freeing the
    guilty, missing a real effect. Its rate (β) depends on the sample size,
    the true effect size and α; the <strong>power</strong> of a test is 1 − β, the
    probability of catching a real effect, and well-designed studies target
    80% or better. The two errors trade against each other (a stricter α
    means more Type II; more data reduces both — the only free lunch).
    <strong>A worked frame:</strong> testing 1,000 drug candidates of which, in truth,
    50 work, at α = 0.05 with 80% power: expect about 950 × 0.05 ≈ 48 false
    "discoveries" alongside 50 × 0.8 = 40 true ones — over half the
    "significant" results are wrong, purely from the arithmetic of
    base rates. This is why <strong>multiple-comparisons</strong> discipline exists:
    test 20 unrelated hypotheses at α = 0.05 and one will "pass" by luck
    alone; corrections (Bonferroni's α/n), pre-registration and replication
    are the antidotes. <strong>p-hacking</strong> — trying analyses, subgroups and
    stopping rules until a p below 0.05 appears — industrialises the same
    leak, and is the main mechanism behind the replication crisis the
    psychology literature documented in the 2010s.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="size the false-discovery harvest" widget="estimate" config={htDiscEst} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "not significant" means "no effect".
    A large, careful trial can return p = 0.2 for a small-but-real effect;
    the correct response is to report the confidence interval on the effect
    ("somewhere between −0.1 and +0.9 points"), which shows precisely what
    the study could and could not rule out. Absence of evidence is not
    evidence of absence — but the interval is what turns that slogan into
    arithmetic.</p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="true or false — reading a p above the bar" widget="judge" config={htSigJudge} />

  <h2>The modern practice: effect sizes, intervals, pre-registration</h2>
  <p>
    Contemporary standards pair every p-value with an <strong>effect size</strong>
    (how big is the difference, in units that matter?) and a <strong>confidence
    interval</strong> (the previous lesson's error bar on it) — because "statistically
    significant but clinically trivial" (a drug that lowers blood pressure by
    0.1 mmHg, p = 0.01, n = 50,000) and "significant and huge" deserve
    different headlines. Pre-registration — declaring hypotheses, sample size
    and analysis plan before seeing data — attacks the multiple-comparison
    and p-hacking leaks at the source, and is now required by many journals.
    The one-line takeaway that survives every methodological fashion: the
    p-value is one number about one comparison under one model; it earns its
    keep only inside a design that controlled everything else.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="two kinds of significant — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={htEffCompare} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a 95% confidence interval means there is a 95% chance the true value lies in the interval you computed. Once calculated, the interval either contains the parameter or it does not — the 95% describes the procedure, not the product: across many studies, intervals built this way capture the truth 95% of the time, and no probability statement attaches to the one in your hands. This is also why a 99% interval is just a wider interval from the same data — more coverage is bought with width, never with certainty.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> modern practice pairs every p-value with an effect size, a
    confidence interval and a pre-registered plan, because a lone p-value under-reports
    its own story. One question remains — the one exams skip and practice cannot: where
    should the 0.05 come from in the first place?
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing alpha: the costs decide</h2>
  <p>
    The 0.05 convention deserves one hard question: why this number? Because
    α is not a statistical constant but a <em>priced decision</em> — the
    exchange rate between the two errors. Screen a blood donation for a
    dangerous virus: a false negative (Type II) contaminates the supply,
    a false positive (Type I) discards safe blood — so α is set very low
    <em>and</em> confirmatory testing runs again, because the costs are
    asymmetric and catastrophic in one direction. Send a spacecraft to
    sample a comet: a false positive wastes a billion-euro mission, so
    evidence standards are extreme. A/B test a button colour: both errors
    cost pennies, so 0.05 is generous and speed wins. The teaching point
    generalises beyond exams: <strong>the significance level belongs to the
    decision, not the mathematics</strong> — and when someone reports a single
    p-value without saying what each error would have cost, they have
    reported the arithmetic while hiding the economics.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="you set α before the data — walk three decisions" widget="branch" config={htAlphaBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the significance level can be chosen after the data — the p-value came out 0.03, so call it significant at 0.05 (or it came out 0.031, so demand 0.01). The error-rate guarantees are prices paid in advance: α controls false alarms only for thresholds declared before seeing the data, and retrofitting the bar to the result is p-hacking with the paperwork omitted. That is why pre-registration fixes the analysis plan, and why "we report the p-value and let readers judge" is a design failure, not humility.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A study reports p = 0.03 for a new teaching method. State precisely what the 0.03 is — and the two things it is not.</li>
    <li>A screening test detects a disease affecting 1 in 100 people 99% of the time, with a 5% false-positive rate. You test positive. Roughly what is your chance of having the disease?</li>
    <li>Why can the significance level never be chosen after seeing the p-value?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) It is the probability of data at least this extreme
    <em>assuming H₀ is true</em> — the surprise of the data under "nothing is going on".
    It is not the probability that H₀ is true (that direction of the conditional needs a
    prior), and not a 97% chance the method works or replicates. (2) About one in six: of
    10,000 people, roughly 99 true positives share the result with roughly 495 false
    positives, so P(disease | positive) ≈ 99 ÷ (99 + 495) ≈ 0.167 — the base rate, not
    the accuracy figures, decides. (3) Because α is a false-alarm price paid in advance:
    its error-rate guarantee holds only for thresholds declared before the data, and
    retrofitting the bar to the result is p-hacking with the paperwork omitted.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A test weighs data against H₀ — the precise "nothing" claim — never the reverse.</li>
    <li>You can reject H₀ or fail to reject it; the null is never proved, only survived.</li>
    <li>A p-value is P(data this extreme | H₀ true): a conditional probability, not the probability that H₀ is false.</li>
    <li>Type I errors reject a true null at rate α; Type II errors miss real effects at rate β; power is 1 − β.</li>
    <li>The two errors trade against each other; only more data reduces both.</li>
    <li>α belongs to the decision, not the mathematics — the cost of each error sets the bar.</li>
    <li>Report effect sizes and intervals alongside every p-value, and declare the analysis before the data arrives.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The test needs data worth testing: <strong>Sampling &amp; Survey Design</strong> is
    where the n behind every p-value comes from, and a badly drawn sample invalidates the
    whole courtroom before step 1. <strong>Confidence Intervals</strong> remains the
    companion piece — interval and test are two views of one mathematics — and
    <strong>Correlation &amp; Regression</strong> aims the same machinery at fitted lines
    instead of means.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The Bayesian alternative.</strong> The courtroom framing has a rival:
    Bayes' theorem computes P(H | data) directly — what you actually want —
    by combining the data's likelihood with a <strong>prior</strong> probability for
    the hypothesis. The cost is the prior (subjective? whose?), and the two
    schools have argued for a century; the practical resolution in most
    fields is coexistence — p-values for regulatory thresholds, Bayesian
    methods where priors are defensible (A/B testing, diagnostic reasoning,
    the disease-test tree done properly).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Choosing the test.</strong> Which p-value machinery applies depends on
    the data's type and size: binomial tests for counts like the coin;
    t-tests for comparing two means; chi-squared tests for categorical
    tables; permutation tests when assumptions wobble — resample the data
    under H₀ thousands of times and see how unusual the real result is.
    The grammar (H₀, α, p) is invariant; the calculator changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Should the 0.05 threshold be retired, lowered, or
    replaced by reporting standards (the "statisticians' 2019 manifesto" said
    retire)? How should journals reward replication? And can machine-learning
    research — where models are tuned against test sets — adopt pre-
    registration at all? The courtroom metaphor will survive them all:
    the question is always how much surprise a "nothing" claim can absorb
    before honest observers stop believing it.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Neyman &amp; Pearson framework; Fisher's significance testing; Wasserstein &amp; Lazar, "ASA Statement on p-values" (2016); OpenStax, Introductory Statistics.</p>

